###2026.07.16.33
### Faster, stable Docker folder startup
- Performance: The Docker page now reuses one primed coherent runtime snapshot instead of replacing it during the first native `loadlist()` pass or issuing a separate preference request.
- Performance: Full Docker runtime details now begin loading only after the lightweight folder view is visible, keeping advanced metadata off the initial render path.
- UI/UX: The Application column holds its cached width while folders, previews, fonts, and host rows settle, then performs one final measured adjustment instead of visibly resizing several times.
- Performance: Folder rendering now yields according to elapsed browser work rather than pausing after every six folders, reducing unnecessary frame delays on faster systems.
- Reliability: Startup row mutations, font events, preference application, and post-render polish are coalesced behind a generation-safe width stabilization gate.
- Test: Adds focused regression coverage for single-bundle startup, deferred detail hydration, stabilized column sizing, and time-budgeted folder rendering.


###2026.07.16.32
### Live Docker action-menu state
- Fix: Container menus now immediately switch between Start, Stop, Pause, and Resume after lifecycle actions without requiring a page refresh.
- Fix: Prevents stale Start actions from being offered for containers that are already running, eliminating the resulting Docker "already running" error.
- Reliability: Native Unraid context menus now resolve lifecycle state from FolderView Plus's canonical runtime cache instead of the original row's immutable inline arguments.
- UI/UX: Already-initialized folder preview popups refresh their status icon and available actions as soon as incremental runtime reconciliation settles.
- Test: Adds stopped-to-started-to-stopped regression coverage for native context menus and live preview popup refreshes.


###2026.07.16.31
### Docker lifecycle spinner cleanup
- Fix: Container status icons no longer keep spinning after a start, stop, pause, resume, or restart action has settled.
- UI/UX: Expanded member rows, hidden folder members, and compact folder previews now immediately restore the correct started, paused, or stopped glyph during incremental reconciliation.
- Reliability: The shared Docker status normalizer clears Unraid's temporary refresh and spin classes before applying the latest runtime state.
- Test: Adds regression coverage proving every incremental member and preview status path removes pending lifecycle animation classes.


###2026.07.16.30
### Docker lifecycle rebuild loop fix
- Fix: Container start, stop, pause, resume, and restart reconciliation can no longer call `loadlist`, including when preference revisions change while the action is settling.
- Fix: Eliminates the repeated native-container flash caused when a rebuild saved expanded-folder state and triggered another revision mismatch on the next retry.
- Performance: Lifecycle refreshes now apply the latest runtime state to existing keyed rows while recording configuration revision changes for diagnostics instead of rebuilding the table.
- UI/UX: Folder rows remain continuously mounted throughout lifecycle actions, preserving expansion, focus, menus, ordering, and scroll position.
- Safety: Non-lifecycle refreshes still rebuild when folder or preference revisions change, preserving structural reconciliation outside the protected action window.
- Test: Extends lifecycle regression coverage to prove preference-revision churn cannot be promoted into a grouped-table rebuild.


###2026.07.16.29
### Stable Docker lifecycle refreshes
- Fix: Starting, stopping, pausing, resuming, or restarting a container no longer exposes Unraid's ungrouped container table while FolderView reloads.
- Performance: Native Unraid and FolderView lifecycle actions now use keyed in-place runtime reconciliation instead of the destructive global `loadlist` callback.
- Reliability: Lifecycle transitions receive three lightweight refresh attempts across two seconds so delayed Docker state changes settle without rebuilding the grouped DOM.
- UI/UX: Expanded folders, menus, focus, scroll position, previews, ordering, and existing rows remain mounted during container state changes.
- Safety: Real folder or preference revision changes still retain the full rebuild path, while transient empty or structurally incomplete runtime responses preserve the current grouped view.
- Diagnostics: Lifecycle callback interception and incremental refresh results are recorded in Docker troubleshooting telemetry.
- Test: Adds regression coverage proving lifecycle callbacks cannot invoke `loadlist` while unrelated structural callbacks remain unchanged.


###2026.07.16.28
### Incremental runtime row updates
- Performance: Docker, VM, and Dashboard live refreshes now diff keyed runtime entities and patch only rows whose state, autostart, update, manager, identity, or folder-label data changed.
- UI/UX: In-place updates preserve expanded folders, focus, menus, previews, current ordering, scroll position, and existing host row elements instead of rebuilding the full runtime list.
- Performance: VM start, stop, pause, and resume actions now apply an immediate incremental refresh plus one delayed confirmation pass.
- Safety: Entity additions or removals and folder or preference revision changes still use the full Unraid list rebuild so membership and ordering cannot become stale.
- Diagnostics: Docker, VM, and Dashboard runtime state now records incremental versus structural reconciliation counts for troubleshooting.
- Test: Adds runtime diff coverage for changed, unchanged, added, and removed entities plus contracts for all three incremental render paths.


###2026.07.16.27
### Standardized plugin requests
- Quality: Routes every FolderView Plus-owned browser request through one shared client while keeping native Unraid Docker, VM, User Scripts, GraphQL, WebSocket, SSE, and logging transports separate.
- Fix: Prevents preference saves, folder mutations, expanded-state writes, and apply-settings operations from being replayed after an ambiguous network failure; reads retain one controlled retry.
- Quality: Standardizes request tokens, trace IDs, encoded query construction, timeouts, JSON parsing, cache controls, abort handling, upload progress, keepalive writes, and normalized HTTP errors.
- Diagnostics: Adds a bounded sanitized request-activity history with endpoint paths, outcomes, status codes, durations, attempts, and trace IDs to support-bundle UI telemetry.
- Compatibility: Preserves icon upload cancellation/progress, unload-safe background synchronization, runtime snapshot polling, and native Unraid action transports during the migration.
- Test: Adds behavior and enforcement coverage that rejects direct plugin-owned Ajax/fetch calls and mutation retry overrides while validating GET retries, URL encoding, diagnostic redaction, and all migrated runtime/editor paths.


###2026.07.16.26
### Coherent runtime snapshots
- Performance: Replaces fragmented Docker, VM, and Dashboard bootstrap reads with one versioned runtime snapshot per type while preserving Docker's fast initial state render and deferred detail hydration.
- Fix: Captures folders, preferences, saved order, and metadata revisions under one configuration lock so a render cannot combine values from different save generations.
- Performance: Adds lightweight conditional checks with deterministic snapshot tokens so unchanged live-refresh polls omit folder and runtime payloads.
- Quality: Derives host order from the snapshot's entity generation and normalizes state and full runtime shapes to the same identity before calculating change tokens.
- Compatibility: Keeps the existing read endpoints as per-field fallbacks if the snapshot endpoint is unavailable or returns an unsupported contract.
- Test: Adds endpoint assembly, schema, conditional polling, revision invalidation, client projection, fallback, script-order, packaging, and cross-runtime regression coverage.


###2026.07.16.25
### Icon asset-pack activation fix
- Fix: Corrects the installed asset-pack helper path so the separately downloaded `1.0.0` icon library is extracted, linked, and available to the modern folder editor.
- Fix: Makes the plugin install block stop and report a failure when icon-pack verification or activation fails instead of continuing to display a successful installation banner.
- Quality: Prints an explicit activation start message and the helper's ready confirmation in the Unraid update window.
- Test: Adds a manifest regression that expands the plugin-directory entity and proves the activation helper resolves to the installed script path.


###2026.07.16.24
### Versioned icon asset pack
- Performance: Moves the 1,982-file third-party icon library out of the frequently rebuilt core plugin archive and into an independently versioned `1.0.0` asset pack.
- Performance: Reuses a checksum-marked extracted icon cache during ordinary plugin updates and rebuilds the RAM cache from the persistent pack after reboot.
- Quality: Verifies the immutable pack with manifest-pinned MD5 and SHA-256 checksums, stages extraction, atomically activates the cache, and keeps the existing third-party icon URLs through a runtime symlink.
- Quality: Adds deterministic asset-pack tooling and release guards that reject source drift, checksum mismatches, unsafe entries, or accidental icon-library inclusion in the core archive.
- Feature: Exposes icon-pack version, checksum, URL, readiness, and link state through runtime metadata and sanitized support-bundle build identity.
- Test: Adds executable regressions for archive contents, checksum rejection, real extraction, atomic activation, cached-pack reuse, endpoint health, packaging exclusion, and support metadata.


###2026.07.16.23
### Atomic batching across folder workflows
- Performance: Creates selected starter-template folders in one transaction instead of issuing and reconciling one request per folder.
- Performance: Applies Setup Assistant folder creation and membership phases as bounded batch requests, with one folder-map write and one refresh per phase.
- Performance: Applies rule-preview assignments across every destination folder in one multi-target request and removes the 40-item request loop from shared bulk assignment.
- Fix: Repairs every affected nested-folder parent link in one batch so a failed tree-integrity request cannot leave only part of the tree repaired.
- Security: Validates all destination folders and rejects conflicting item destinations before mutation, preventing partial moves when any target is invalid.
- Quality: Uses one configuration lock, one folder-map read, one atomic write, and one Docker-order reconciliation for multi-target assignments while preserving legacy single-target API compatibility.
- Quality: Records sanitized target, assignment, changed-folder, and duration counts for troubleshooting without logging folder or member names.
- Test: Adds executable regressions proving one-revision multi-folder commits, full rollback on conflicts or missing targets, and one request for assignments larger than the former 40-item chunk size.

###2026.07.16.22
### Atomic configuration imports and deletes
- Performance: Replaces one HTTP request and full configuration rewrite per folder with one bounded server-side batch transaction for imports and bulk deletes.
- Performance: Applies all folder changes under one configuration lock with one folder-map read, one normalization pass, one atomic folder-map write, one metadata commit, and one manual-order reconciliation.
- Performance: Removes the fixed import and delete completion delays so the refreshed result appears as soon as the transaction finishes.
- Security: Validates every delete, update, and create operation before changing disk state, preserves the existing pre-import safety backup and undo flow, and attempts an in-lock rollback if a post-write step fails.
- Quality: Reconciles nested-folder parents, manual folder order, and Docker start order once after the final batch state is known instead of repeating that work for every folder.
- Quality: Records sanitized batch counts and server execution time for troubleshooting without exporting folder names or payload content.
- Test: Adds an executable transaction regression proving multi-folder changes commit one folder revision, repair hierarchy and order correctly, and reject invalid batches without partial mutation.

